The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
u8 *acks = skb->data + sizeof(struct rxrpc_wire_header) + sizeof(struct rxrpc_ackpacket);
if (nr_acks > 0) skb_condense(skb);
if (i == 256) { acks -= i; i = 0; }
```

Reason: `rxrpc_input_soft_acks()` directly uses `skb->data` after `skb_condense()`, which can silently fail to linearize a fragmented or cloned skb, causing out-of-bounds or incorrect reads. The flawed rollover logic (reset `acks` pointer and loop index at 256) also mishandles extended SACK tables by overwriting the buffer offset.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
u8 sack[256] __aligned(sizeof(unsigned long));
u8 *acks = sack;
memset(sack, 0, sizeof(sack));
nsack = umin(sp->ack.nr_acks, 256);
if (skb_copy_bits(skb, sizeof(struct rxrpc_wire_header) + sizeof(struct rxrpc_ackpacket), sack, nsack) < 0) return;
// replaced: if (i == 256) { acks -= i; i = 0; }
if ((i & 255) == 0) acks = sack;
// removed: if (nr_acks > 0) skb_condense(skb);
```

Reason: `skb_copy_bits()` safely copies the SACK table from the possibly fragmented skb into a local stack buffer, ensuring contiguous data. The rollover now resets `acks` to the start of the local buffer at each 256‑entry boundary (handling extended ACKs correctly). The `skb_condense()` call is removed because the copy eliminates the need for linearization.

